ing in the 17th Maine Infantry, from 1862 to 1865."}, "D2S5": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0325-2641b4896c35:d01", "start": 328, "end": 489, "text": "He was in private practice in Portland, Maine from 1866 to 1867, in West Union, West Virginia from 1868 to 1870, in Parkersburg, West Virginia from 1870 to 1874."}, "D2S6": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0325-2641b4896c35:d01", "start": 490, "end": 568, "text": "He was a Prosecuting attorney of Wood County, West Virginia from 1874 to 1878."}, "D3S1": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0325-2641b4896c35:d02", "start": 0, "end": 9, "text": "Luther F."}, "D3S2": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0325-2641b4896c35:d02", "start": 10, "end": 180, "text": "Cole Luther Francis Cole (October 25, 1925 – July 26, 2013) was a lawyer and politician from Baton Rouge, Louisiana, who served as a state legislator and then as a judge."}, "D3S3": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0325-2641b4896c35:d02", "start": 181, "end": 281, "text": "Cole was born in Alexandria in Rapides Parish, one of twelve children of Clement and Catherine Cole."}, "D3S4": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0325-2641b4896c35:d02", "start": 282, "end": 376, "text": "He graduated from Bolton High School and served in the United States Navy during World War II."}, "D3S5": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0325-2641b4896c35:d02", "start": 377, "end": 497, "text": "He attended Louisiana Tech University in Ruston and graduated from Louisiana State University Law Center in Baton Rouge."}, "D3S6": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0325-2641b4896c35:d02", "start": 498, "end": 576, "text": "He joined a friend, Warren Mengis, in the formation of the Cole and Mengis law"}, "D4S1": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0325-2641b4896c35:d03", "start": 0, "end": 147, "text": "him by more than three decades, dying in Providence on 21 June 1811 in her 87th year; she was buried in Saint John's Church Cemetery in Providence."}, "D4S2": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0325-2641b4896c35:d03", "start": 148, "end": 193, "text": "Cole's immediate ancestry is found in John O."}, "D4S3": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0325-2641b4896c35:d03", "start": 194, "end": 243, "text": "Austin's Genealogical Dictionary of Rhode Island."}, "D4S4": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0325-2641b4896c35:d03", "start": 244, "end": 318, "text": "The ancestry of Cole's grandmother, Susanna Cole, was published by John D."}, "D4S5": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0325-2641b4896c35:d03", "start": 319, "end": 345, "text": "Champlin in 1913 and 1914."}, "D4S6": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0325-2641b4896c35:d03", "start": 346, "end": 522, "text": "The Dexter ancestry of Cole's mother is from the Dexter genealogy (1905), supplemented with more recent scholarship from \"The Great Migration\" series as cited within the chart."}, "D4S7": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0325-2641b4896c35:d03", "start": 523, "end": 612, "text": "\"Online sources\" John Cole (judge) John Cole (1715–1777) was a lawyer who became the 12th"}}, "candidates": [{"candidate_id": "C0", "answer": "May 22, 1841", "card_ids": ["ramdocs-0325-2641b4896c35:d00:c0"]}, {"candidate_id": "C1", "answer": "January 1, 1900", "card_ids": ["ramdocs-0325-2641b4896c35:d01:c0"]}, {"candidate_id": "C2", "answer": "October 25, 1925", "card_ids": ["ramdocs-0325-2641b4896c35:d02:c0"]}, {"candidate_id": "C3", "answer": "1715", "card_ids": ["ramdocs-0325-2641b4896c35:d03:c0"]}], "original_cards": [{"doc_id": "ramdocs-0325-2641b4896c35:d00", "card_id": "ramdocs-0325-2641b4896c35:d00:c0", "entity": "Justice Cole", "scope": "Birth date", "claim": "Charles Cleaves Cole was born on May 22, 1841.", "answer": "May 22, 1841", "quote": "Charles Cleaves Cole Charles Cleaves Cole (May 22, 1841 – March 17, 1905) was a United States federal judge.", "original_document_number": 1}, {"doc_id": "ramdocs-0325-